{"name":"Bucket hat","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Bucket_hat_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucket_hat_(cropped).jpg","caption":"Bucket hat (cropped)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["The bucket hat is a soft hat with a downward-sloping brim all the way round. It began as a wool or tweed hat for rain in Ireland, was later issued in cotton to US troops, and became a street-fashion staple through hip-hop and later catwalk revivals.\nFor looks it works as a styling piece: it changes the silhouette of the head, can cover a hairline or hairstyle, and gives casual outfits a streetwear feel. Its brim also shades the face and scalp."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People wear it with casual outfits, festival clothing and outdoor wear. Materials range from cotton and nylon to wool. Wearers choose a size that sits at the forehead and decide whether to wear the brim down or flipped up."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["It carries no medical risks. Wearing hats in general can compress hair and some people note hat-hair; the brim offers shade but is not a substitute for sunscreen."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Bucket_hat_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e4/Bucket_hat_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Bucket hat (cropped)","license":"CC BY 3.0","creator":"JJagomagi","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucket_hat_(cropped).jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Polo_Bucket_Hat.jpg/960px-Polo_Bucket_Hat.jpg?utm_source=commo